# Recorded artifact bytes are output, not source. Git must not touch them.
#
# `npm run check:vectors` hashes every file under `vectors/` and compares the digest to the
# one recorded on the day that vector was written (see `vectors/README.md` and ADR-0015).
# `coverage-report.csv` is RFC 4180 CSV and carries CRLF line terminators, so under git's
# ordinary text handling the committed blob loses them: measured on this repository with
# `core.autocrlf=input`, the worktree file hashed to the recorded
# c3c88ba5696c79ebed948de65a1d96d722522e35d488a7ef57f9cdf01b546881 while the staged blob held
# zero carriage returns. A fresh clone would then fail the gate on a byte the library never
# changed -- and on a machine configured the other way it would fail differently. `-text`
# turns end-of-line conversion off for the corpus so a checkout is the bytes that were
# recorded, everywhere.
/vectors/** -text
